  "query_preview": "..\n/Issued 527 AM MDT Mon Sep 21 2026/ \n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nAn upper level ridge centered over MT and surface high pressure \nover the northern US plains will maintain very light flow aloft and \nat the surface across the region through tonight. Except for some \nareas of fog this morning, primarily across the plains but also in \nsome SW MT valleys, mainly clear skies and light winds will prevail \nwith afternoon temperatures reaching the 70s. A southwesterly flow \ndevelops Tuesday through the rest of the week, bringing some \nadditional warming with afternoon temperatures as much as 10-15 \ndegrees above average through Thursday. A weakening upper level\ndisturbance tracking out of the Southwestern US across WY by mid-\nweek will bring risk for showers and perhaps an isolated thunderstorm\nto portions of SW MT late Tue/Tue night. A larger scale upper \ntrough from the North Pacific begins to move onshore Friday with a\ncold front sweeping east across the Northern Rockies and MT late \nFriday or early Saturday. Gusty winds are likely to accompany and \nfollow the frontal system along with cooler temperatures by the \nweekend.\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nLooking ahead to the late week/weekend system, there is reasonable \nmodel agreement on a potent shortwave ejecting across the Northern \nRockies, which would favor a fairly energetic frontal passage and \nassociated winds. Probabilistic model guidance keeps the risk for \nstrong (>50mph) winds around 20% less, and there is still a fair \namount of timing uncertainty, but this will continue to be monitored \nas the week progresses. Hoenisch.",
